        "falseColor": false,
        "notes": "No image of this planet's surface exists: it is an unresolved point in every telescope, seen by its own infrared glow at 1,740 K, some 3 arcseconds' worth of orbit from its star at most. Its size, 1.45 Jupiter radii, comes from its measured luminosity through evolutionary models, not from a measured disc (see the ledger). The sphere turns once every 9.00 hours, the period JWST measured from its brightness changes, about an axis on its orbit normal, where its spin is measured to point. The gray marks an unresolved surface."
      }
